\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Vasili Pukirev, \emph  {The Unequal Marriage} (Wikipedia). Philosophers of mathematics have often treated mathematical beauty like a daughter they never wanted, and felt that pairing her up with something more ``respectable'' and ``rational'' would be the best one could hope for.}}{297}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Cain, Figure 9.8. A string is wrapped along the circular arc $ADC$. When unwound its endpoint traces the involute $AE$. The tangent $EF$ of the involute is determined by $FD/DE=EB/BD$. An ugly formula, but it becomes beautiful, Leibniz argues in an unpublished essay ``On the Beauty of Theorems,'' when one re-conceives it in terms of a continuous motion through the corresponding sequence of points $FDEBD$ (much in the manner of the pattern unlock feature on Android phones). Such a compressed mental representation of a complex relationship is an example of what is called ``chunking'' in modern cognitive psychology.}}{298}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Cain, Figure 3.11. Archimedes reportedly discovered thirteen semi-regular solids. This could likely mean that his definition included the rhombicuboctahedron (left) but excluded the pseudo-rhombicuboctahedron (right), which has an ``ugly'' twist or asymmetry in it. ``If Archimedes was guided by aesthetic judgement or a sense of symmetry, his classification is complete; if he was guided by a technical definition, it is incomplete.'' This is an example of Cain finding hidden aesthetic themes behind dry historical facts.}}{298}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
